some pretty mixed opinions tonight about  the proposed clark county school  district transition plan  the 205 page document was posted online  just last night and it outlines how to  bring  staff and students back in a hybrid  model a combination of  at home and in person learning but as  kristen drummond shows us  it seems to raise more questions and  give answers  now it's all going to get shaken up  again a logistical shakeup  one more set of massive changes many  remain uncertain about the ccsd  transition plan  the proposal divides students into three  groups two participate in a mix of  in-person and at-home learning  while the other cohort is full-time  distance education  i think the biggest concern is the  specifics are not there  carmen andrews is a ccsd teacher she  says this recommendation  causes concerns among educators it's  left very open to interpretation  and the way teachers read it is we are  responsible for everything  a big worry the hybrid instructional  model educators may be assigned to  teaching students in person  and online as well possibly at the same  time  the hybrid plan ends up in like triple  work for teachers there's still  not a lot of clarity regarding exactly  what the different groups of kids are  going to be doing  and what level of support they'll be  receiving rebecca garcia with the nevada  parent teacher association  also questions the secondary school  schedule students on campus  only attend two courses they must  complete the other half of the school  day  online whoever thinks that's going to  happen i mean like as a mom i'm like  yeah my kid's going to come home from  in-person school and log right back in  probably not  but others welcomed the district taking  this initiative i think they have a  really really  good plan in motion a plan to try and  reopen soon  kristin drummond 8 news now the board of  trustees will vote on the reopening plan  on thursday of course that plan also  contingent on any changes the governor  could make if cases don't go down with  the new stay-at-home order  in the next two weeks well the union  representing ccsd support staff released  a statement about that plan today  and the education support employees  association president  says at this point the timeline  presented by the district and their  reopening plan  is not workable with all of the changes  that have yet to be made to ensure staff  and  students are safe in our schools
 

